-- ================================================================
-- Ramsey R(3,3) = 6: Constructive Proof in Kore
-- ================================================================
--
-- Theorem: R(3,3) = 6
--   (A) R(3,3) > 5: exhibit a 2-coloring of K₅ with no mono triangle
--   (B) R(3,3) ≤ 6: pigeonhole argument (mathematical, not computational)
--
-- K₅ edge index (upper triangle, 0-based):
--   edge(i,j) for i<j: flat index in row-major upper triangle
--   (0,1)=0  (0,2)=1  (0,3)=2  (0,4)=3
--   (1,2)=4  (1,3)=5  (1,4)=6
--   (2,3)=7  (2,4)=8
--   (3,4)=9
-- ================================================================

-- Stdlib
: fst unpair drop ;
: snd unpair swap drop ;
: abs dup 0 < if neg end ;
: sum 0 [ + ] fold ;

-- ============================================================
-- Part A: C₅ witness — verify no monochromatic triangle
-- ============================================================
-- C₅ cycle: 0→1→2→3→4→0 (red=0), diagonals (blue=1)
-- Coloring indexed by edge:
--   [0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0]

-- Verify: cycle edges sum to 0 (all red)
-- Cycle edges: (0,1)=0, (1,2)=4, (2,3)=7, (3,4)=9, (0,4)=3
( 0 1 1 0 0 1 1 0 1 0 )   -- coloring
dup 0 get  over 4 get +  over 7 get +  over 9 get +  over 3 get +
-- Stack: coloring cycle_sum
-- cycle_sum should be 0 (all red)
swap

-- Verify: diagonal edges sum to 5 (all blue)
-- Diagonal edges: (0,2)=1, (0,3)=2, (1,3)=5, (1,4)=6, (2,4)=8
dup 1 get  over 2 get +  over 5 get +  over 6 get +  over 8 get +
-- Stack: cycle_sum coloring diag_sum
swap drop  -- drop coloring

-- Stack: 0 5
-- 0 = all cycle edges red ✓
-- 5 = all diagonal edges blue ✓
--
-- Why this means no monochromatic triangle:
-- Every triangle in K₅ contains either 1 or 2 cycle edges
-- (never 0 or 3), because C₅ is triangle-free and its
-- complement is also triangle-free (it's also C₅).
-- Therefore every triangle has mixed colors. QED Part A.

-- ============================================================
-- Part B: R(3,3) ≤ 6 — pigeonhole (mathematical proof)
-- ============================================================
-- For any 2-coloring of K₆, pick any vertex v.
-- v has 5 edges. By pigeonhole, ≥3 have the same color c.
-- Let those 3 neighbors be {a,b,c}.
-- 
-- Case 1: Some edge in {a,b,c} has color c
--   → That edge + v forms a monochromatic triangle of color c
-- Case 2: No edge in {a,b,c} has color c
--   → All 3 edges in {a,b,c} have color (1-c)
--   → {a,b,c} is a monochromatic triangle of color (1-c)
--
-- In either case, a monochromatic triangle exists. QED Part B.
-- Combined: R(3,3) = 6. ∎

-- ============================================================
-- Result: Stack contains [0, 5] proving the C₅ coloring is valid
-- 0 = cycle edges are monochromatic (all red)
-- 5 = diagonal edges are monochromatic (all blue)  
-- Together: valid 2-coloring, and by C₅ structure, triangle-free
-- ============================================================
